    <h2>Mathematical Model of Aspect Scaling and Gaussian Background Synthesis</h2>
    <p>
      When adapting an arbitrary source image of dimensions <code>(W_s, H_s)</code> onto a target canvas <code>(W_t, H_t)</code> without cropping, the scaling factor <code>S</code> is determined by the minimum dimensional ratio:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>Scale = min( W_t / W_s,  H_t / H_s )</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      The centered bounding box coordinates <code>(X_offset, Y_offset)</code> for the pristine source graphic satisfy:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>X_offset = ( W_t - (W_s * Scale) ) / 2,   Y_offset = ( H_t - (H_s * Scale) ) / 2</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      To fill the peripheral canvas area <code>Area_peripheral = (W_t * H_t) \ Area_source</code>, the engine scales the source image to cover the canvas and applies a two-dimensional discrete Gaussian blur convolution kernel:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>G(x, y) = (1 / (2 * pi * sigma^2)) * exp( - (x^2 + y^2) / (2 * sigma^2) )</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      Where <code>sigma = 45.0</code> pixels. The resulting ambient color field mirrors the exact chroma, saturation, and atmospheric tones of your AI composition without harsh boundaries or distracting repetition.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked Example: Resizing a Square 1024px DALL-E Portrait to a 1920x1080 YouTube Banner</h2>
